  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.971,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.085,
            0.701,
            0.102
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "On passing excess carbon dioxide the following reaction takes place:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.105,
            0.536,
            0.141
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_2.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} CaCO _{3}( s ) +  H _{2} O ( l ) +  CO _{2}( g ) \\rightarrow  Ca ( HCO _{3})_{2}( aq ) \\\\( Soluble in water )\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.157,
            0.723,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Limestone, chalk and marble are different forms of calcium carbonate. All metal carbonates and hydrogencarbonates react with acids to give a corresponding salt, carbon dioxide and water.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.206,
            0.515,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, the reaction can be summarised as –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.233,
            0.828,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Metal carbonate/Metal hydrogencarbonate + Acid → Salt + Carbon dioxide + Water",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.272,
            0.676,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.1.4 How do Acids and Bases React with each other?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.317,
            0.296,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 2.6"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.352,
            0.698,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take about 2 mL of dilute NaOH solution in a test tube and add two drops of phenolphthalein solution.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.382,
            0.446,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the colour of the solution?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.396,
            0.646,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Add dilute HCl solution to the above solution drop by drop.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.411,
            0.595,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is there any colour change for the reaction mixture?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.426,
            0.698,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why did the colour of phenolphthalein change after the addition of an acid?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.455,
            0.583,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now add a few drops of NaOH to the above mixture.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.469,
            0.583,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Does the pink colour of phenolphthalein reappear?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.483,
            0.476,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why do you think this has happened?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.523,
            0.722,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the above Activity, we have observed that the effect of a base is nullified by an acid and vice-versa. The reaction taking place is written as –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.559,
            0.479,
            0.576
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{NaOH(aq)+HCl(aq)\\rightarrow NaCl(aq)+H_{2}O(l)}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.579,
            0.723,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The reaction between an acid and a base to give a salt and water is known as a neutralisation reaction. In general, a neutralisation reaction can be written as –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.631,
            0.374,
            0.647
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm { B a s e } + \\mathrm { A c i d } \\rightarrow \\mathrm { S a l t } + \\mathrm { W a t e r }"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.662,
            0.578,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.1.5 Reaction of Metallic Oxides with Acids"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.694,
            0.296,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 2.7"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.729,
            0.697,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a small amount of copper oxide in a beaker and add dilute hydrochloric acid slowly while stirring.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.759,
            0.698,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note the colour of the solution. What has happened to the copper oxide?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.801,
            0.723,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You will notice that the colour of the solution becomes blue-green and the copper oxide dissolves. The blue-green colour of the solution is due to the formation of copper(II) chloride in the reaction. The general reaction between a metal oxide and an acid can be written as –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.866,
            0.433,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Metal oxide + Acid → Salt + Water",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.916,
            0.271,
            0.93
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Acids, Bases and Salts"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
